=== SURVEY ===

Baseline non-AI behavior preserved by this slice:

1. Backend route GET /api/v1/maintenance returns a deterministic
   list of maintenance items per-vehicle. Source:
   internal/api/maintenance_handler.go. The handler reads
   persistent items from the maintenance_items table when present,
   and falls back to a hard-coded default list (Cabin Air Filter,
   Tire Rotation, Wiper Blades) derived from the current odometer
   read via the WithRedisCache(*signal.RedisSignalCache) signal
   cache. Items carry the legacy field names current_mileage,
   due_mileage, interval_miles (pre-Phase-48 SI cutover —
   maintenance.go was not migrated to SI in this slice because
   touching SI-canonicalization code is out of allowed-files scope).

2. Backend route GET /api/v1/service-records returns the recent
   service-record history per vehicle. Source:
   internal/api/maintenance_handler.go::GetServiceRecords.

3. Frontend route /maintenance is owned by
   web/src/features/vehicle-systems/pages/MaintenancePage.tsx
   (686 lines pre-edit). The page composes (in order top to bottom):
   - PageHeader with the active vehicle's display name
   - Summary metric cards (FadeIn): total, due-soon, overdue,
     completed counts derived from the maintenance items
   - Filter / Sort toolbar with category select + sort select +
     "Schedule Maintenance" CTA
   - Maintenance items grid (status + due_date + due_mileage +
     progress against interval bar) — the canonical reminders
   - Recent service records table (date, description, mileage,
     cost) with running cost summary
   - Cost-summary FadeIn aggregating service-record totals.

4. Threshold reminders: every item carries a derived status
   (overdue / due-soon / good) computed by the page from the
   item's due_date OR due_mileage relative to today and the
   current odometer. The status drives the status badge colour
   and the order in which items surface in the grid.

5. Settings → AI page already exposes an explicit per-feature
   toggle gate: when ai_mode='off' the entire AI surface is
   hidden, and per-feature toggles independently gate each
   individual feature's UI + routes (ADR-015 §I7).

Why the baseline must stay: the prompt's central invariant is
that the deterministic threshold reminders are the canonical
maintenance reminder source. Operators with ai_mode='off' (the
default) MUST continue to see the exact same list of items, the
same status badges, the same summary metric cards, the same
Schedule Maintenance write path, and the same service-record
table. The AI advisor adds an OPT-IN narrative panel; it never
mutates a maintenance item, never reorders the grid, and never
substitutes for the deterministic reminders.

Closest existing slice precedent reviewed before edits:
0048 state-machine-debugger-narrator (the most recent
RAG-narrator slice with scope binding, two read-only tools,
PolicyDigest redaction, and AIFeatureCard wiring). The 0049
slice mirrors that template structurally so future maintainers
can recognise the pattern at a glance.

DTOs and types involved:
- Backend body: AIPredictiveMaintenancePredictRequest{
    VehicleID int64 `json:"vehicle_id"`
  } — only one field, parsed via json.Decoder +
  DisallowUnknownFields. Per-field validation rejects
  vehicle_id <= 0.
- Tool envelope: MaintenancePredictionContextEnvelope with
  per-item fields (id, category, name, status, due_date,
  due_mileage, current_mileage, last_service_date, interval_*,
  derived_status) + recent_records + summary aggregate counts.
- Scope binding: ScopedMaintenancePredictionWindow{
    VehicleID int64
  } — vehicle-only (maintenance items aren't time-windowed,
  unlike 0048's window-bounded FSM trace).

Registry routes for predictive-maintenance:
- Backend:   POST /api/v1/ai/maintenance/predict
- Frontend:  /maintenance
- UITestIDs: ai-feature-predictive-maintenance-root
- JobNames:  ai_maintenance_model_update
- PushKinds: ai_maintenance_alert

=== REASONING ===

ADR-015 alignment:

§I1 default-off — registry entry sets DefaultOn=false; the
guard's per-feature gate is false unless the user explicitly
turns it on in Settings → AI. The aigen-generated TS mirror
web/src/ai/features.ts carries the same default. No env var
or migration ships this feature on.

§I3 baseline intact — the MaintenancePage edit ONLY appends an
opt-in FadeIn block (between the summary metric cards FadeIn and
the Filter / Sort toolbar FadeIn) wrapping <AIPredictiveMaintenance/>.
The threshold reminders, status badges, summary metric cards,
upcoming items list, recent service records table, cost-summary
FadeIn, and Schedule Maintenance CTA are unchanged. The
withAiFeature HOC returns null when ai_mode='off' OR when the
per-feature toggle is false, so the appended FadeIn renders no
DOM in off mode (proven by the React test, which asserts the
container is empty).

§I4 zero egress — in off mode useAiStream is never instantiated
because withAiFeature short-circuits to null BEFORE the inner
section's hooks run. The backend guard returns 404 before
reaching the dispatcher. No provider call is ever made.

§I5 hidden UI — the wrapping div from withAiFeature carries
data-testid="ai-feature-predictive-maintenance-root" only when
the feature is enabled; the React off-mode test asserts the
test ID is absent from the DOM AND that the visible "Predict
maintenance" verb is absent from the accessible name.

§I6 404 routes — POST /api/v1/ai/maintenance/predict is mounted
inside the same guarded scope as every other AI route in
ai_routes.go (g.Wrap("predictive-maintenance", …)). The
off-mode 404 invariant is therefore enforced by the existing
guard matrix (proven for every AI feature by the foundation
slice 0001's matrix tests, not duplicated per-slice).

§I7 per-feature — the guard checks the per-feature toggle
INDEPENDENTLY of ai_mode. The React off-mode test's second
case (ai_mode='cloud' AND toggle=false) proves the surface is
still hidden.

§I8 propose-only — the AI surface NEVER mutates maintenance
items, service records, signal-pipeline entry points, or
reminder thresholds. The advisor is read-only narration over
the deterministic maintenance envelope. There is no "Apply to
form" or "Use this draft" action — the render contract is
"narrative", not "proposal" or "suggestion".

§I10 type system — the handler body is parsed with
json.Decoder + DisallowUnknownFields; vehicle_id is typed
int64. The MaintenancePredictionContextEnvelope and
ScopedMaintenancePredictionWindow are exhaustive typed structs
with no map[string]any. The only place a legacy
map[string]interface{} survives is in the production
AIPredictiveMaintenanceContextSource, which mirrors the
upstream MaintenanceHandler.defaultItems return shape — that
shape is part of the existing pre-Phase-48 maintenance handler
contract and re-typing it would require touching
maintenance_handler.go (out of allowed-files scope).

§I12 client/bg — JobNames=[ai_maintenance_model_update] and
PushKinds=[ai_maintenance_alert] are declared in registry
metadata so the 9999 AI-OFF contract walker has coverage data.
NO actual worker code or push-delivery pipeline ships in this
slice. This mirrors the digest-narration ai_digest_weekly and
nl-search ai_search_indexer precedents, where the worker /
push pipeline landed in a follow-up slice. The
service-worker chunk ai-predictive-maintenance is reserved
for future route-split work and is not written to by this
slice.

Methodology principles P1-P12:

P1 provider port — the strategy uses the shared provider.Registry;
no direct adapter import escapes feature code.

P4 strategy / dispatcher — the strategy declares allowedTools
[query_maintenance_context, retrieve_maintenance_chunks]; the
shared dispatcher loop is unchanged.

P5 decorator chain — every provider call routes through the
locked decorator chain (redaction, rate limit, cost cap, audit,
trace) installed once at boot.

P6 backend guard + frontend gate — POST /api/v1/ai/maintenance/predict
is wrapped with g.Wrap("predictive-maintenance", …); the React
component is wrapped with withAiFeature('predictive-maintenance', …).

P8 eval goldens — 3 goldens in goldens.yaml (typical_overdue_item,
typical_healthy_baseline, refusal_out_of_scope_request) each
backed by a canned mock-provider YAML in canned/*.yaml. The
eval harness passes 3/3.

P9 feature registry — the registry entry declares Routes with
ALL five fields populated explicitly (Backend, Frontend,
UITestIDs, JobNames, PushKinds), not omitted, per the
methodology's "empty arrays must be explicit, not omitted" rule.

P10 baseline coexistence — selection between baseline and AI
happens at composition time. MaintenancePage always renders the
deterministic reminders; the AIPredictiveMaintenance card is
appended via withAiFeature, which returns null in off mode. There
is no per-call branch inside business logic; the canonical
maintenance handler is reached for both paths.

P11 wired-or-absent — the AIPredictiveMaintenance component
imports useAiStream and calls it unconditionally at the top of
InnerSection's body (Hooks-rules safe). The hook's URL is the
registered backend endpoint /ai/maintenance/predict (the
request() client that useAiStream wraps prepends /api/v1).

P12 no placeholder buttons — the action button's disabled prop is
the COMPUTED expression `!canStart` derived from
`typeof vehicleId === 'number' && Number.isFinite(vehicleId) && vehicleId > 0`,
NOT a literal `disabled` or `disabled={true}`. The On-mode
wiring test asserts the button is enabled when vehicleId is set
AND disabled when missing / zero / negative / non-finite (four
input states). No "future slice", "coming soon", "wiring lands",
or "would call POST" strings appear in the component (W1 inline
self-check returns 0).

Tools (F4 typed-DTO contract):
- query_maintenance_context (input: vehicle_id int64) returns
  the typed MaintenancePredictionContextEnvelope. The tool
  REFUSES any LLM-supplied vehicle_id that doesn't match the
  scope bound at handler entry — defends against prompt-injection
  exfiltration via operator-authored service-record notes.
- retrieve_maintenance_chunks (input: query string, optional
  top_k) calls the F7 retriever scoped to the allowlist
  [maintenance_event, vehicle_state, ml_anomaly]. The tool
  has NO vehicle_id input — separation of concerns is handled
  by the F7 retriever's subject-scoping and the source-type
  allowlist. TestRetrieveMaintenanceChunks_InputSchemaOmitsVehicleID
  pins this contract.

Redaction (F8 PolicyDigest):
- Allowed classes: ClassVehicleName only.
- Round-trip required: yes (the strategy emits the policy ID
  in its system message; the redaction layer round-trips the
  digest IDs back to the originals before the response reaches
  the operator).
- VINs, lat/long, place names, IPs, emails, phone numbers,
  MAC addresses, and IDs in plaintext are redacted before the
  message reaches the provider.

Scope binding (per-request):
The handler installs ScopedMaintenancePredictionWindow{VehicleID}
into the request context BEFORE the dispatcher runs. The
query_maintenance_context tool reads the scope from the context
and refuses any LLM-supplied vehicle_id that doesn't match. This
is vehicle-only (no time window) because maintenance items are
not time-bounded — different from 0048's
ScopedFSMTraceWindow{VehicleID, FromUnix, ToUnix}.

User-prefs / units:
This slice does NOT duplicate userPrefsMiddleware. The
deterministic envelope already emits SI-canonical fields
alongside the legacy unit-suffixed fields where the upstream
MaintenanceHandler exposes them; the dispatcher's user-prefs
seed message gives the LLM the conversion formulas it needs to
narrate in the operator's display units.

